    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d134">

                <pb facs="0147" n="135" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg135"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/9592: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-03T17:00:00+03:00">April 3, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>667. In the course of conversation last night with Sobolev,<anchor n="47"
                        xml:id="d134-f1"/> the Secretary General of the Commissariat for Foreign
                    Affairs, who was more communicative than usual, he expressed the view that the
                    situation in the Balkans had reached a crisis and that “something would happen
                    within the next few days”. He remarked that the Yugoslavs were good fighters and
                    that he expected them to defend themselves unless internal dissension prevented
                    an organized defense.</p>
                <p>When I remarked that I assumed he had heard the rumors of a possible German
                    attack on the Soviet Union he replied that not only would such an attack be
                    “madness” but that he could not conceive of any reason therefor as “this was no
                    time” for the Germans to create a second front. When I replied that the German
                    attack on Stavanger, Bergen and Narvik could have been considered “madness” and
                    that there was no “front” in western Europe he remarked that he still could find
                    no “adequate reason” for a German attack on the Soviet Union particularly with
                    “conditions as they are in the Balkans”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d134-f1" n="47" xml:id="d134-f1-content">
                <p>Arkady Alexandrovich Sobolev.</p>
            </note>
